(Per-function unit tests. The deterministic gate (tests/220_determinism.vox)
 covers generation-as-a-whole; this file covers the pieces a generated
 finding would be reported through, and the one security-sensitive seam.

 Three groups:
  - 'shell quote' (harness.vox): the single audit point for every
    user-controlled value that reaches a shell. The six cases below are the
    ones that distinguish quoting from concatenation -- an embedded single
    quote, an embedded double quote, a space, a newline, an empty string,
    and a string that is only quotes. A broken quote would splice an
    unescaped value into a 'run shell' string; pinning the exact bytes here
    means the one audit point stays audited.
  - 'rng below' (rng.vox): at the two boundary bounds (0 and 1), and that it
    stays in 0..n-1 over a thousand draws. rng below 0 is a modulo-by-zero
    edge; Vox evaluates it to 0, which is in-bounds, and that is the
    behavior worth pinning so a future change to modulo does not turn the
    fuzzer's seed reproduction into a divide-by-zero.
  - 'finding path' (findings.vox): an odd category and a large seed, to
    pin that the path is built by text concatenation (not a shell), so an
    attacker-controlled category name could never become a shell argument.
  - 'classify compile exit' (loop_gen.vox): the exit-code -> category map,
    extracted as a pure function so it can be called directly here. The
    128/129 boundary is the point: 128 is a legitimate exit code, 129 is a
    signal death, and a naive `>= 128` would misclassify a real exit-128 as
    a crash.)

see "../src/rng.vox".
see "../src/harness.vox".
see "../src/findings.vox".
see "../src/runner.vox".
see "../src/gen_literals.vox".
see "../src/gen_manifest.vox".
see "../src/gen_core.vox".
see "../src/gen_buffers.vox".
see "../src/gen_files.vox".
see "../src/gen_collections.vox".
see "../src/gen_text.vox".
see "../src/gen_things.vox".
see "../src/gen_flow.vox".
see "../src/gen_misc.vox".
see "../src/loop_gen.vox".

(shell quote: hoisted to locals because the format string cannot nest a
 "..." literal inside a {...} interpolation. The call itself takes a text
 argument, so the literal belongs at the call site, not in the Print.)
a text called q1 is 'shell quote' of "O'Brien".
a text called q2 is 'shell quote' of "say \"hi\"".
a text called q3 is 'shell quote' of "a b".
a text called q4 is 'shell quote' of "a\nb".
a text called q5 is 'shell quote' of "".
a text called q6 is 'shell quote' of "'''".
Print "quote O'Brien: {q1}".
Print "quote say-hi: {q2}".
Print "quote space: {q3}".
Print "quote newline: {q4}".
Print "quote empty: {q5}".
Print "quote quotes: {q6}".

(rng below at the two boundary bounds. below 1 is always 0; below 0 is the
 modulo-by-zero edge, evaluated to 0.)
'rng seed' of 7.
Print "rng below 1: {'rng below' of 1}".
Print "rng below 0: {'rng below' of 0}".

(rng below stays in 0..n-1 over a thousand draws. 'bad' counts any draw
 outside that range; it must stay 0. Uses only is less than / is greater
 than, both proven elsewhere, so the assertion itself cannot be the thing
 that fails compiling.)
'rng seed' of 42.
a number called bad is 0.
a number called i is 0.
While i is less than 1000,
    a number called r is 'rng below' of 100,
    If r is less than 0 then, Set bad to bad add 1.
    If r is greater than 99 then, Set bad to bad add 1.
    Set i to i add 1.
Print "rng bounds: bad={bad} (0 means all 1000 draws of below 100 stayed in 0..99)".

(finding path: an odd category and a large seed. The result is plain text
 concatenation -- no shell -- which is the property that makes a category
 name safe even if it were attacker-controlled.)
a text called fp is 'finding path' of "odd-cat" and 999999999.
Print "finding path: {fp}".

(classify compile exit: the exit-code -> category map. Inlined in the
 interpolation because the argument is a number, not a string literal, so
 there is no nested-"..." trap. -1 is built from 0 minus 1 so the literal
 is not a leading dash, which the compiler rejects.)
a number called neg is 0 minus 1.
Print "classify 0: {'classify compile exit' of 0}".
Print "classify 1: {'classify compile exit' of 1}".
Print "classify 101: {'classify compile exit' of 101}".
Print "classify 128: {'classify compile exit' of 128}".
Print "classify 129: {'classify compile exit' of 129}".
Print "classify -1: {'classify compile exit' of neg}".
(The prelude's name pools must stay disjoint from each other and from
 every other pool that declares a TOP-LEVEL name. Type names, function
 names, flag names, the buffer names 'gen buffer name' hands out and the
 value names 'gen fresh noun' hands out all land in Vox's one identifier
 space (LANGUAGE.md:1569), so one word in two pools is a program that
 does not compile - and it would surface as a rare, seed-dependent
 compile failure rather than as anything obviously wrong with the lists.
 Counted here so a word added to one list cannot silently collide.

 The buffer-versus-value row is NOT zero: "the parcel" is in both, which
 predates this test and is recorded in the prelude report rather than
 quietly fixed here. It is printed rather than asserted so it stays
 visible until someone adjudicates it.

 The lengths are printed too, because 'gen cycled word' takes a drawn
 stride and only visits every word before repeating when the length is
 PRIME. A word added to one of these lists without checking that is a
 silent duplicate name, which is the same compile failure by another
 route.)

To 'times a pool holds' with a list called 'the pool' and a text called 'the word'.
    a number called 'the hits' is 0.
    a number called 'how many' is 'the pool's length.
    a number called 'the position' is 1.
    While 'the position' is less than or equal to 'how many',
        a text called 'the candidate' is element 'the position' of 'the pool',
        If 'the candidate' is 'the word' then,
            Set 'the hits' to 'the hits' add 1.
        Set 'the position' to 'the position' add 1.
    Return a number, 'the hits'.

To 'words in common' with a list called 'the first pool' and a list called 'the second pool'.
    a number called 'the overlap' is 0.
    a number called 'how many first' is 'the first pool's length.
    a number called 'the outer' is 1.
    While 'the outer' is less than or equal to 'how many first',
        a text called 'the word' is element 'the outer' of 'the first pool',
        a number called 'the hits' is 'times a pool holds' of 'the second pool' and 'the word',
        Set 'the overlap' to 'the overlap' add 'the hits',
        Set 'the outer' to 'the outer' add 1.
    Return a number, 'the overlap'.

Print "pool lengths: types={gen_type_words's length} functions={gen_function_words's length} flags={gen_flag_words's length} fields={gen_field_words's length} makers={gen_maker_words's length} receivers={gen_receiver_words's length} parameters={gen_parameter_words's length}".
Print "types vs functions: {'words in common' of gen_type_words and gen_function_words}".
Print "types vs flags: {'words in common' of gen_type_words and gen_flag_words}".
Print "functions vs flags: {'words in common' of gen_function_words and gen_flag_words}".
Print "types vs buffers: {'words in common' of gen_type_words and gen_buffer_words}".
Print "functions vs buffers: {'words in common' of gen_function_words and gen_buffer_words}".
Print "flags vs buffers: {'words in common' of gen_flag_words and gen_buffer_words}".
Print "types vs value nouns: {'words in common' of gen_type_words and 'the value nouns'}".
Print "functions vs value nouns: {'words in common' of gen_function_words and 'the value nouns'}".
Print "flags vs value nouns: {'words in common' of gen_flag_words and 'the value nouns'}".
Print "types vs value verbs: {'words in common' of gen_type_words and 'the value verbs'}".
Print "functions vs value verbs: {'words in common' of gen_function_words and 'the value verbs'}".
Print "flags vs value verbs: {'words in common' of gen_flag_words and 'the value verbs'}".
Print "buffers vs value nouns (pre-existing, not mine): {'words in common' of gen_buffer_words and 'the value nouns'}".

(No prelude word may CONTAIN one of tests/200_never_emitted.vox's banned
 needles. That guard scans generated source for the substring, so a name
 that merely embeds one trips it: "amount" contains "mount" and did,
 failing the safety guard at seed 2 the first time these pools grew.
 200 catches it across a 2000-program sweep; this catches it in the one
 place it can be fixed, and says which word.)

To 'the word holds' with a text called 'the word' and a text called needle.
    a buffer called 'the word bytes' is "{'the word'}".
    a buffer called 'the needle bytes' is "{needle}".
    a number called 'how long' is 'the word bytes's size.
    a number called 'how long a needle' is 'the needle bytes's size.
    a number called 'the start' is 1.
    a boolean called 'it is here' is false.
    While 'the start' is less than or equal to 'how long',
        a boolean called 'this one matches' is 'the word matches at' of 'the word bytes' and 'the start' and 'the needle bytes' and 'how long a needle' and 'how long',
        If 'this one matches' then,
            Set 'it is here' to true.
        Set 'the start' to 'the start' add 1.
    Return a boolean, 'it is here'.

To 'the word matches at' with a buffer called 'the word bytes' and a number called 'the start' and a buffer called 'the needle bytes' and a number called 'how long a needle' and a number called 'how long'.
    a number called 'the end' is 'the start' add 'how long a needle' minus 1.
    If 'the end' is greater than 'how long' then,
        Return a boolean, false.
    a number called 'the step' is 0.
    a boolean called 'still matching' is true.
    While 'the step' is less than 'how long a needle',
        a number called 'the word position' is 'the start' add 'the step',
        a number called 'the needle position' is 'the step' add 1,
        a number called 'the word byte' is byte 'the word position' of 'the word bytes',
        a number called 'the needle byte' is byte 'the needle position' of 'the needle bytes',
        If 'the word byte' is not 'the needle byte' then,
            Set 'still matching' to false.
        Set 'the step' to 'the step' add 1.
    Return a boolean, 'still matching'.

To 'banned needles in' with a list called 'the pool'.
    a list called 'the needles' is ["shutdown", "poweroff", "reboot", "restart", "halt", "mount", "pivot", "device node"].
    a number called 'the hits' is 0.
    a number called 'how many words' is 'the pool's length.
    a number called 'how many needles' is 'the needles's length.
    a number called 'the word position' is 1.
    While 'the word position' is less than or equal to 'how many words',
        a text called 'the word' is element 'the word position' of 'the pool',
        a number called 'these hits' is 'banned needles in one' of 'the word' and 'the needles' and 'how many needles',
        Set 'the hits' to 'the hits' add 'these hits',
        Set 'the word position' to 'the word position' add 1.
    Return a number, 'the hits'.

To 'banned needles in one' with a text called 'the word' and a list called 'the needles' and a number called 'how many needles'.
    a number called 'the hits' is 0.
    a number called 'the needle position' is 1.
    While 'the needle position' is less than or equal to 'how many needles',
        a text called needle is element 'the needle position' of 'the needles',
        a boolean called 'it is here' is 'the word holds' of 'the word' and needle,
        If 'it is here' then,
            Set 'the hits' to 'the hits' add 1.
        Set 'the needle position' to 'the needle position' add 1.
    Return a number, 'the hits'.

Print "banned needles in types: {'banned needles in' of gen_type_words}".
Print "banned needles in fields: {'banned needles in' of gen_field_words}".
Print "banned needles in makers: {'banned needles in' of gen_maker_words}".
Print "banned needles in receivers: {'banned needles in' of gen_receiver_words}".
Print "banned needles in functions: {'banned needles in' of gen_function_words}".
Print "banned needles in parameters: {'banned needles in' of gen_parameter_words}".
Print "banned needles in flags: {'banned needles in' of gen_flag_words}".
